## Issues for joint adjudication

1. Noncompactness remains stated without the finite/totally disconnected qualification identified by ER006/ER007.
2. The blanket claim that the time-T map is compact on the whole phase space is too broad; compactness is for bounded solution families under uniform derivative bounds.
3. Step 2 invokes a compact closure of the union of images of an open neighborhood; that closure need not be compact in an infinite-dimensional phase space.
4. Quantitative `C1 O(epsilon)` closeness needs uniform second-derivative or `C1,1` control; `Ck` plus localization may suffice only after the localization is correctly established.
5. The exact Bates–Lu–Zeng theorem and theorem number still need verification; the response may mix semiflow and map variants.
6. The map-to-semiflow uniqueness argument is plausible only if `Phi^s(M_epsilon)` remains in the same admissible local manifold class uniformly for `s∈[0,T]`; the response does not state that uniqueness theorem precisely.
7. It incorrectly calls the result “forward and backward invariance” under a semiflow. Only forward invariance/equality for nonnegative times is meaningful unless a flow on the manifold is separately constructed.
8. Local injectivity of the projected embedding does not alone prove global injectivity. Compact-domain `C1` stability of embeddings can repair this, but the response merely asserts injectivity.
9. The hypothesis table marks complete base invertibility as verified for any compact finite-dimensional invariant RFDE manifold; this requires a cited theorem or must remain an assumption.
10. The slack characteristic stability is used as though uniform in all delay values; only the declared parameter point/range is justified unless proved otherwise.
11. The direct Hopf theorem claims a generic periodic branch without assuming a nonzero first Lyapunov coefficient or equivalent nonlinear nondegeneracy.
12. Rouché on a vertical strip is unnecessary and insufficiently specified; local spectral isolation around the simple pair is enough.
13. At parity, `O(1)` coupling removes a perturbative guarantee but does not necessarily “destroy” the actual spectral gap.
14. The characteristic-matrix notation for general RFDE derivatives should be stated as the derivative functional evaluated on exponential histories.
15. The publication-ready theorem is stronger than what the supplied proof safely establishes because several localization, theorem-matching, embedding, and nonlinear Hopf conditions remain implicit.

## Comparison with internal proof

ER009 provides a useful stronger hypothesis template—especially inverse tangent control—that should inform a later revision of the internal conditional theorem. The internal proof remains more conservative on:

- full semiflow invariance;
- quantitative `C^r` closeness;
- vertical projection;
- nonlinear Hopf branch status.

Both agree that finite-time tracking is the only unconditional result and concrete A021 NHIM hypotheses remain unverified.